&gt;&gt; Sir, Mr. Mayor, members of the council,

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:59.520 align:start position:19%
thank you for the opportunity to speak.
My name is Gary Pew. I'm a resident of

00:05:57.039 --> 00:06:03.680 align:start position:19%
Cheyenne. I live in Ward One.
I'm here today to oppose Chapter 1.28

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:07.360 align:start position:19%
administrative warrants because
administrative warrants raise serious

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:11.440 align:start position:19%
constitutional concerns and directly
affect the residents of our city. This

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.960 align:start position:19%
issue is not abstract and it's not
partisan. It goes to the heart of the

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.080 align:start position:19%
Fourth Amendment in the relationship
between local communities and government

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.520 align:start position:19%
authority. The Fourth Amendment
guarantees that people are secure in

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:26.000 align:start position:19%
their homes and that the government may
not search or seize without a warrant

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:31.199 align:start position:19%
issued by an independent impartial judge
based on probable cause. That safeguard

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:35.759 align:start position:19%
exists for one reason to ensure that no
government agency can act as judge and

00:06:33.199 --> 00:06:39.600 align:start position:19%
enforcer at the same time.
Administrative warrants violate that

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.880 align:start position:19%
principle. Unlike judicial warrants,
administrative warrants are issued

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:47.600 align:start position:19%
internally by government agencies.
Remember, a municipal judge is appointed

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:51.759 align:start position:19%
by the mayor and the council.
Administrative warrants are not reviewed

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:55.919 align:start position:19%
or approved by an independent judge.
When an agency authorizes its

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:59.919 align:start position:19%
enforcement actions, the constitutional
check designed to protect the public is

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:04.960 align:start position:19%
removed. That is exactly the kind of
unchecked authority the Fourth Amendment

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:08.639 align:start position:19%
is written to prevent. The Supreme Court
has consistently held that home deserves

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:13.039 align:start position:19%
the highest level of constitutional
protection. Entering a private residence

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:15.840 align:start position:19%
without consent generally requires a
judicial warrant. Administrative

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:20.000 align:start position:19%
warrants simply do not meet that
standard. They bypass judicial oversight

00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:24.000 align:start position:19%
and weaken the constitutional barrier
that protects residents from

00:07:21.199 --> 00:07:28.560 align:start position:19%
unreasonable searches and seizures. This
matter is a local is at a local level

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:32.800 align:start position:19%
because constitutional violations do not
occur in theory. They occur on our

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:36.240 align:start position:19%
streets and in our doorsteps. Entering a
private residence without consent

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:41.780 align:start position:19%
generally requires a judicial warrant.
Admin administrative warrants simply do

00:07:38.319 --> 00:07:44.560 align:start position:19%
not meet that standard.
[clears throat] When residents fear that

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:48.160 align:start position:19%
the government agents enter their home
without an independent judge's approval,

00:07:46.319 --> 00:07:51.840 align:start position:19%
trust erodess. People become less
willing to cooperate with law

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:57.520 align:start position:19%
enforcement, report crimes, or engage
with local institutions. That loss of

00:07:54.160 --> 00:08:00.560 align:start position:19%
trust harms public safety for everyone.
Some argue that administrative warrants

00:07:59.039 --> 00:08:03.680 align:start position:19%
are necessary for efficiency, but
efficiency has never been a

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:07.199 align:start position:19%
constitutional justification. The
Constitution was intentionally designed

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:11.680 align:start position:19%
to slow the government down when
fundamental rights are at stake.

00:08:09.199 --> 00:08:16.000 align:start position:19%
Convenience cannot override due process.
This is a slippery precedent. If

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:20.080 align:start position:19%
agencies are permitted to bypass
independent judicial warrants in one

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:24.879 align:start position:19%
area of enforcement, that authority can
expand. Rights that depend on a person

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:29.039 align:start position:19%
targeted are not rights at all. The
Fourth Amendment either protects

00:08:26.319 --> 00:08:33.599 align:start position:19%
everyone in this city or it protects no
one. City councils are responsible to

00:08:31.759 --> 00:08:37.360 align:start position:19%
uphold constitutional values and protect
their communities. Speaking out against

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:41.519 align:start position:19%
the use of administrative warrants is
not obstructing the law. It is about

00:08:39.839 --> 00:08:45.680 align:start position:19%
insisting that the law be enforced
within constitutional limits. I urge

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:49.279 align:start position:19%
this council to affirm its commitment to
the Fourth Amendment, to judicial

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:53.680 align:start position:19%
oversight, and to the principle that no
government agency should have the power

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:55.680 align:start position:19%
to police itself at the expense of our
residents rights. Thank you for your

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:00.000 align:start position:19%
time.
&gt;&gt; You did almost exactly three minutes.

00:08:57.279 --> 00:09:05.200 align:start position:19%
Thank you, Mr. Pew.
Representative, welcome to our meeting.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:09.680 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Mr. Mayor.
My name is Anne Lucas and I do not live

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:14.480 align:start position:19%
in the city of Cheyenne. I live in the
county, but I am the house district rep.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:20.160 align:start position:19%
I am the house rep for district 43,
which encompasses a fair little bit of

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:24.640 align:start position:19%
the city of Cheyenne and I have heard
from many constituents and some of whom

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:30.560 align:start position:19%
are here this evening and it's great to
see you folks here. Um, so I just have a

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:35.200 align:start position:19%
few comments. Mr. Mayor and council
members, I want to be really clear on

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:41.440 align:start position:19%
this. I fully support the city's ability
to act immediately when there is a real

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:46.959 align:start position:19%
emergency. Fires, gas leaks, structural
collapse, or other situations that can

00:09:43.839 --> 00:09:51.920 align:start position:19%
put lives at risk. I also believe that
laws should be enacted when they must be

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:55.920 align:start position:19%
enacted and not because we can enact
them. And I don't think that this

00:09:53.839 --> 00:10:01.360 align:start position:19%
ordinance is limited to emergent
situations.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:06.399 align:start position:19%
I looked at the title 15 and um there
are three chapters that don't apply in

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:12.800 align:start position:19%
this ordinance and they happen to be the
historic design guideline,

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:17.040 align:start position:19%
solar access permit system and house
numbers

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:24.000 align:start position:19%
and every other bit of the building code
applies in this ordinance including um

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:29.360 align:start position:19%
energy conf conservation code. This is a
a bill about code code code enforcement.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:34.959 align:start position:19%
Um,
none of these really are emergencies or

00:10:32.399 --> 00:10:38.800 align:start position:19%
are so seldom emergencies. I if if it
was, you could still do it because it

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:44.399 align:start position:19%
would be an emergency.
A missing permit, a failed inspection

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:48.399 align:start position:19%
signoff, an unapproved alteration, or a
long-standing maintenance issue doesn't

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:54.000 align:start position:19%
suddenly become a life-threatening
situation because simply because an

00:10:50.399 --> 00:10:58.240 align:start position:19%
inspector can't gain access.
Yet, this ordinance allows inter

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:02.160 align:start position:19%
administrative inspection warrants to be
used for all of title 15 with the

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:08.640 align:start position:19%
exception of those three chapters that I
me mentioned. This is the problem. If

00:11:05.519 --> 00:11:13.760 align:start position:19%
the issue is truly ex exigent, a fire
hazard, a gas leak, a building is going

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:18.079 align:start position:19%
to fall down and kill somebody, we
already have the ex the required

00:11:16.399 --> 00:11:23.279 align:start position:19%
authority to deal with those
emergencies. You don't have to go and

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:28.560 align:start position:19%
get a warrant to put out a fire or keep
people from getting killed. So, this is

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:33.440 align:start position:19%
an exigent. The city should always be
able to require a judicial warrant

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:38.800 align:start position:19%
reviewed by a judge with probable cause
and the scope clearly defined. Th that

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:43.760 align:start position:19%
is not an unreasonable burden. It is a
constitutional baseline. This ordinance

00:11:41.519 --> 00:11:49.040 align:start position:19%
blurs the line between emergency
response and ordinary enforcement. It

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:54.399 align:start position:19%
treats routine compliance matters as if
they justify the same intrusion as a

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:58.079 align:start position:19%
life safety emergency.
And they don't.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:02.160 align:start position:19%
Private property rights are not an
inconvenience to be worked around.

00:12:00.079 --> 00:12:07.600 align:start position:19%
They're a boundary that requires care,
restraint, and judicial oversight. I

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:13.440 align:start position:19%
urge you to amend this ordinance so that
immediate access to private property is

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:15.920 align:start position:19%
limited strictly to genuine safety
emergencies.

00:12:14.959 --> 00:12:19.519 align:start position:19%
All
&gt;&gt; you understand your time is up.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:22.240 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Oh, I'm not used to that.
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you guys do it to me all the time

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:26.160 align:start position:19%
when I come to They do it all the time
to me at the legislature to say you're

00:12:23.839 --> 00:12:29.360 align:start position:19%
up and sit down, Mr. Mayor. So,
&gt;&gt; well, somebody would say point of order,

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:32.320 align:start position:19%
but I understand and I appreciate your
time, folks. Thank you very much.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:37.243 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Made your point. I really appreciate you
being here this evening. Thank you.

00:12:35.223 --> 00:12:37.243 align:start position:19%
[snorts]

00:12:38.959 --> 00:12:43.760 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Good evening.
&gt;&gt; Good evening, mayor and member members

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:47.440 align:start position:19%
of the council. My name is Kathy
Sigliano and I am a resident here in

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:52.000 align:start position:19%
Laramie County. I'd like to speak in
opposition to the proposed ordinance.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:56.880 align:start position:19%
Last week, three of you from a committee
of four agreed to postpone and I have

00:12:54.399 --> 00:13:01.440 align:start position:19%
received emails from Councilman Moody,
Esabel White, as well as Councilwoman

00:12:59.519 --> 00:13:05.600 align:start position:19%
Emmens and Aldridge saying that they
would support support a postponement.

00:13:03.839 --> 00:13:09.360 align:start position:19%
When I asked in an email back if
amendments and revisions would be

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:13.680 align:start position:19%
submitted to you all by today's meeting
if you would still vote to postpone, I

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:19.040 align:start position:19%
only received a response of yes from
Michelle Aldridge. The postponement was

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:23.440 align:start position:19%
not conditional. I am asking that if a
revision is given, you still postpone to

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:28.639 align:start position:19%
allow public comment to give feedback
and address any concerns they may have.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:33.120 align:start position:19%
I I ask you give at least more than a
few hours. Two weeks would be nice. But

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:37.600 align:start position:19%
in event you still move to vote tonight,
I will say this. History has a funny way

00:13:35.279 --> 00:13:42.560 align:start position:19%
of repeating itself, especially when
people convenient convince themselves

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:48.399 align:start position:19%
that the time right now is okay for the
government to be used in a gently,

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:53.040 align:start position:19%
responsible, or only on the right people
time of matter. The British crown

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:57.519 align:start position:19%
thought the same thing in the 18th
century when it issued general warrants,

00:13:55.440 --> 00:14:02.480 align:start position:19%
vague permissions that allowed officials
to search homes, businesses, and

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:07.199 align:start position:19%
property without sus specific suspicion.
No names, no clear limits, no real

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:11.680 align:start position:19%
accountability, just trust. Those
warrants were widely unpopular,

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:15.680 align:start position:19%
routinely abused, and became one of the
very reasons we have the Fourth

00:14:13.199 --> 00:14:19.199 align:start position:19%
Amendment. Our founders didn't add
protections against unreasonable

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:23.199 align:start position:19%
searches because they were paranoid or
dramatic. They did it because they had

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:28.880 align:start position:19%
lived under a system where government
agents could enter property first,

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:33.600 align:start position:19%
justify it later. This ordinance walks
dangerously close to that same line.

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:38.959 align:start position:19%
Calling these warrants noncriminal
doesn't make them harmless. A forced

00:14:36.079 --> 00:14:43.839 align:start position:19%
entry onto private property is still a
search. Allowing unelected officials to

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:49.519 align:start position:19%
obtain warrants based on broad standards
like inspection or safety concerns

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:53.440 align:start position:19%
without owner consent or clear probable
cause is exactly the kind of authority

00:14:51.839 --> 00:14:57.600 align:start position:19%
that the fourth amendment was designed
to restrain.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:15:00.720 align:start position:19%
And what we are seeing here is a blatant
disregard to this community by refusing

00:14:59.279 --> 00:15:05.399 align:start position:19%
to take into consideration any
amendments or suggestions from the

