details. But long story short, with this

01:00:20.799 --> 01:00:26.720 align:start position:19%
one, uh, what we're doing is we are
proposing to remove the owner occupancy

01:00:25.040 --> 01:00:32.079 align:start position:19%
requirement. So right now if you have an
accessory dwelling unit you have to have

01:00:29.520 --> 01:00:36.720 align:start position:19%
that primary land owner living there and
then they can have a second unit and

01:00:34.559 --> 01:00:42.240 align:start position:19%
that unit can be occupied by a family
member. It can be occupied by a renter,

01:00:39.119 --> 01:00:48.640 align:start position:19%
another family, anybody. Um when that is
developed um they are developed in a

01:00:45.920 --> 01:00:54.240 align:start position:19%
fashion that allows for a little bit
less building code um adherence because

01:00:51.680 --> 01:00:58.160 align:start position:19%
you have an owner occupied structure. Um
what this does is this removes that

01:00:55.920 --> 01:01:03.040 align:start position:19%
owner occupied requirement which means
that your new um [clears throat] two

01:01:01.119 --> 01:01:07.760 align:start position:19%
unit your accessory dwelling unit will
be built to a little bit different of a

01:01:04.720 --> 01:01:11.119 align:start position:19%
standard. Uh for example the uh two
units will have to have the ability to

01:01:09.599 --> 01:01:15.119 align:start position:19%
independently turn off the water if
there's a water leak a little valve you

01:01:13.359 --> 01:01:18.880 align:start position:19%
would put on there. Um or also they
would have to be able to control heat

01:01:17.520 --> 01:01:23.839 align:start position:19%
you know in their units instead of
having the upstairs one um control the

01:01:21.040 --> 01:01:27.440 align:start position:19%
downstairs one and vice versa. Um, what
this really does for us is it really

01:01:25.359 --> 01:01:35.839 align:start position:19%
does eliminate a lot of the issues that
we see when these these two homes

01:01:32.000 --> 01:01:39.680 align:start position:19%
get sold and then the realtor says,
"Hey, this is a two-unit. You can rent

01:01:37.920 --> 01:01:45.680 align:start position:19%
both of them out." We typically run into
a lot of problems with those. Also, what

01:01:42.240 --> 01:01:51.440 align:start position:19%
this does is it allows for what's
commonly referred to as flat over flat

01:01:48.880 --> 01:01:55.280 align:start position:19%
housing units. What does that mean? That
basically means you would have a house

01:01:53.280 --> 01:01:59.359 align:start position:19%
on top with a basement apartment. So
that's how is referenced here in the

01:01:56.720 --> 01:02:03.760 align:start position:19%
code. One one other item that is or two
other items that are very important with

01:02:01.440 --> 01:02:07.920 align:start position:19%
with this uh proposed change is it
changes the way that parking is uh

01:02:06.079 --> 01:02:12.079 align:start position:19%
required to be provided on the site.
It's not waving the parking requirement.

01:02:09.920 --> 01:02:17.200 align:start position:19%
It simply is saying that if you have a
two, three car driveway, you can utilize

01:02:14.480 --> 01:02:20.240 align:start position:19%
that driveway for parking purposes. The
way it is phrased right now is you are

01:02:18.880 --> 01:02:24.000 align:start position:19%
required to provide an independent
parking space. So this allows more

01:02:22.240 --> 01:02:29.599 align:start position:19%
creativity and flexibility for these
housing units um in the community.

